<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sip Song Chau Tai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sip <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Chau Tai ('twelve Tai lands') was the traditional name for the Tai principalities of the Black River region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Each was ruled by hereditary Tai chiefly families, such as the Deo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-chau">Lai Chau</a>, which at various times paid tribute to Vietnamese, Lao or Chinese courts. Under French rule the region became the basis of the Tai Federation of 1948–1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sip-song-chau-tai.jpg" alt="Sip Song Chau Tai" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tai_Dam_People.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
